// An anchor point of a vector path.
//
// TWO SPACES, and it is the most important rule of the whole message -- the
// renderer, the hit-test and the pen tool must read it in exactly the same way,
// in Go and in TypeScript (web/src/store/vectorGeometry.ts is the only
// implementation).
//
//  1. `x`/`y` are in NODE-LOCAL coordinates: the origin is (Node.x, Node.y), so
//     the point in world coordinates is (Node.x + x, Node.y + y).
//     NOT in world coordinates, and it is not a whim: every existing consumer
//     works on the node's box -- selectTool sends a setProps{x,y} to drag, the
//     overlay draws the selection from x/y/width/height, the renderer discards a
//     node with width/height <= 0. With anchors in world coordinates, dragging a
//     vector node would move the box and leave the path where it was, unless ALL
//     the geometry were rewritten on every move (one more rule that nobody
//     applies). With local anchors, the geometry travels with the node for
//     free -- and it will stay that way when track 1 makes coordinates relative
//     to the parent: a vector inside a group will move with the group without
//     one extra line.
//
//  2. `in_`/`out_` are OFFSETS RELATIVE TO THE ANCHOR (not points): the control
//     point of the curve that ENTERS the anchor is (x + in_x, y + in_y), the one
//     of the curve that EXITS it is (x + out_x, y + out_y).
